   "cite": "orthogonal-basis-def",
   "prompt": "<p>Consider the vectors $(1,2,3)$, $(-3,0,1)$, $(1,-5,3)$.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Check that the vectors are linearly independent and so form a basis of ${\\mathbb{R}}^3$.</li><li>Check that the vectors are mutually orthogonal, and are therefore an orthogonal basis.</li><li>Represent $(1,1,1)$ as a linear combination of this basis.</li><li>Make the basis orthonormal.</li></ol>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "solution": "a) The matrix with these vectors as columns, $\\left[ \\begin{smallmatrix} 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3 \\end{smallmatrix} \\right]$, has determinant $70 \\neq 0$ (equivalently, row reduction finds a pivot in every column), so the three vectors are linearly independent, and three linearly independent vectors in ${\\mathbb{R}}^3$ form a basis. b) $\\langle (1,2,3), (-3,0,1) \\rangle = -3 + 0 + 3 = 0$, $\\langle (1,2,3), (1,-5,3) \\rangle = 1 - 10 + 9 = 0$ and $\\langle (-3,0,1), (1,-5,3) \\rangle = -3 + 0 + 3 = 0$, so every pair is orthogonal. c) The coefficients are $a_j = \\frac{\\langle \\kvecx, \\kvecv_j \\rangle}{\\langle \\kvecv_j, \\kvecv_j \\rangle}$ with $\\kvecx = (1,1,1)$: $\\frac{6}{14} = \\frac{3}{7}$, $\\frac{-2}{10} = -\\frac{1}{5}$ and $\\frac{-1}{35}$, so $(1,1,1) = \\frac{3}{7}(1,2,3) - \\frac{1}{5}(-3,0,1) - \\frac{1}{35}(1,-5,3)$. d) Divide each vector by its magnitude, $\\sqrt{14}$, $\\sqrt{10}$ and $\\sqrt{35}$: $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}(1,2,3)$, $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}(-3,0,1)$, $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{35}}(1,-5,3)$."
   }
  },
  {
