   },
   "cite": "gram-schmidt-start",
   "prompt": "<p>Starting with $(1,2,3)$, $(1,1,1)$, $(2,2,0)$, follow the Gram–Schmidt process to find an orthogonal basis of ${\\mathbb{R}}^3$.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "solution": "$\\kvecw_1 = (1,2,3)$, with $\\langle \\kvecw_1, \\kvecw_1 \\rangle = 14$. Next, $\\langle (1,1,1), \\kvecw_1 \\rangle = 6$, so $\\kvecw_2 = (1,1,1) - \\frac{6}{14}(1,2,3) = \\left(\\frac{4}{7}, \\frac{1}{7}, \\frac{-2}{7}\\right)$, with $\\langle \\kvecw_2, \\kvecw_2 \\rangle = \\frac{3}{7}$. Last, $\\langle (2,2,0), \\kvecw_1 \\rangle = 6$ and $\\langle (2,2,0), \\kvecw_2 \\rangle = \\frac{10}{7}$, so $\\kvecw_3 = (2,2,0) - \\frac{3}{7}(1,2,3) - \\frac{10}{3}\\left(\\frac{4}{7}, \\frac{1}{7}, \\frac{-2}{7}\\right) = \\left(\\frac{-1}{3}, \\frac{2}{3}, \\frac{-1}{3}\\right)$. The orthogonal basis is $(1,2,3)$, $\\left(\\frac{4}{7}, \\frac{1}{7}, \\frac{-2}{7}\\right)$, $\\left(\\frac{-1}{3}, \\frac{2}{3}, \\frac{-1}{3}\\right)$."
   }
