   "cite": "gram-schmidt-start",
   "prompt": "<p>Find an orthogonal basis of ${\\mathbb{R}}^3$ such that $(3,1,-2)$ is one of the vectors. Hint: First find two extra vectors to make a linearly independent set.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "solution": "One answer: $(3,1,-2)$, $(1,0,0)$, $(0,1,0)$ are linearly independent (the determinant of the matrix they form is $-2 \\neq 0$), so run the Gram–Schmidt process on them. $\\kvecw_1 = (3,1,-2)$, $\\langle \\kvecw_1, \\kvecw_1 \\rangle = 14$; $\\kvecw_2 = (1,0,0) - \\frac{3}{14}(3,1,-2) = \\left(\\frac{5}{14}, \\frac{-3}{14}, \\frac{3}{7}\\right)$; $\\kvecw_3 = (0,1,0) - \\frac{1}{14}(3,1,-2) + \\frac{3}{5}\\left(\\frac{5}{14}, \\frac{-3}{14}, \\frac{3}{7}\\right) = \\left(0, \\frac{4}{5}, \\frac{2}{5}\\right)$. Scaled, the basis $(3,1,-2)$, $(5,-3,6)$, $(0,2,1)$ is orthogonal: the three inner products are $15 - 3 - 12 = 0$, $0 + 2 - 2 = 0$ and $0 - 6 + 6 = 0$. Any orthogonal basis containing $(3,1,-2)$ is an answer."
